---
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';
import LineChart from '../components/LineChart.astro';
import v01 from '../../public/data/runs/p1_01_forward_validation.json';
import tt from '../../public/data/runs/p1_02_traveltime.json';
import SectionMap from '../components/SectionMap.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const st = v01.stokes;
const ra = v01.rayleigh;
const rc = v01.reciprocity;
const ab = v01.absorbing;
const run = v01.manifest;
const ray10 = ra.rows.find((r) => r.points_per_wavelength === 10)!;
const cx = tt.surveys.crosshole;
const su = tt.surveys.surface;
const chamber = { x0: -3, x1: 3, y0: -18, y1: -12 };
const holesXZ = (cx.stations.receivers as number[][]).filter((p) => Math.abs(p[1]) < 0.3).map((p) => [p[0], p[2]] as [number, number]);
const surfXZ = (su.stations.receivers as number[][]).filter((p) => Math.abs(p[1]) < 0.3).map((p) => [p[0], p[2]] as [number, number]);
const pct = (v: number) => `${v > 0 ? '+' : ''}${(v * 100).toFixed(1)}%`;
---
<Doc
  title="Seismic tomography, simulated"
  kicker="Phase 1 · the reference ceiling"
  current="seismic"
  lede="Before asking whether a satellite can see a chamber, ask what the instruments built for the job can see. Geophysicists image the shallow ground with arrays of geophones and a source, hammer blows or the ambient hum, and invert the records into a three-dimensional tomogram. This chapter does that in simulation, over ground whose contents are known."
  state="status · composition model and test benches done · 3-D elastic solver validated · inversions in progress"
  index={[
    { id: 'chain', label: 'The chain' },
    { id: 'forward', label: 'Forward model' },
    { id: 'traveltime', label: 'Travel times' },
    { id: 'arrays', label: 'Arrays' },
    { id: 'methods', label: 'Three methods' },
    { id: 'scoring', label: 'Scoring' },
  ]}
>
  <section id="chain">
    <h2>Every tomogram here is scored against a truth the method never saw</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The ground is composed first, from sourced materials. The waves are computed through it by a solver that knows
      nothing about any inversion. The inversions see only what the sensors recorded, with noise, on a different grid.
      Whatever they draw is then compared with the chambers that are really there.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">compose</span>
        <h3>Known ground</h3>
        <p>
          Test benches from one chamber to the claimed underworld, and Giza. Strata, sand cover, water table and von
          Kármán heterogeneity, all as data. <a href={`${base}/underworld/`}>See them in the underworld.</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">simulate</span>
        <h3>Waves through it</h3>
        <p>3-D isotropic elastic velocity–stress finite differences, fourth order in space, absorbing boundaries on the sides and floor, a free surface on top, air-filled chambers as traction-free cavities.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">recover</span>
        <h3>What practitioners would draw</h3>
        <p>Travel-time tomography, ambient-noise tomography and full-waveform inversion, each run as it would be run in the field, then scored voxel by voxel.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="forward">
    <h2>The solver matches exact solutions to within a fraction of a percent</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Before it touches a chamber, the forward model is checked where the answer is known exactly, in Mokattam limestone
      (P {v01.manifest.params.vp_m_s.toLocaleString('en-US')} m/s, S {v01.manifest.params.vs_m_s.toLocaleString('en-US')} m/s).
    </p>
    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{st.worst_ncc.toFixed(3)}</span><span class="what">worst correlation with the exact point-force response, every component</span><span class="src">Stokes · {st.points_per_wavelength.toFixed(0)} points per wavelength</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{(100 * st.worst_amplitude_error).toFixed(1)}<small>%</small></span><span class="what">worst amplitude error against the same solution</span><span class="src">Stokes</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{ray10.error_percent.toFixed(2)}<small>%</small></span><span class="what">Rayleigh-wave speed error on the free surface at ten points per S wavelength</span><span class="src">Rayleigh equation</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{rc.ncc.toFixed(4)}</span><span class="what">reciprocity: swapping source and receiver across a {rc.void_m} m void</span><span class="src">reciprocity</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{ab.final_relative.toExponential(0).replace('e-', '×10⁻').replace(/(\d+)$/, (d) => [...d].map((c) => '⁰¹²³⁴⁵⁶⁷⁸⁹'[+c]).join(''))}</span><span class="what">of the peak energy left in the grid once the waves have gone</span><span class="src">absorbing boundaries</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      {
        st.panels.map((p) => (
          <LineChart
            title={`Point force: ${p.component} velocity, ${p.receiver}, ${p.offset_m.toFixed(0)} m`}
            series={[
              { name: 'exact', x: p.t_ms, y: p.exact, color: 'var(--text)', width: 1.5 },
              { name: 'simulated', x: p.t_ms, y: p.simulated, color: 'var(--verdigris)', dash: '4 3' },
            ]}
            xLabel="time, ms"
            yLabel="velocity (scaled)"
            height={170}
            zeroLine
            directLabels={false}
            caption={`correlation ${p.ncc.toFixed(4)} · amplitude ratio ${p.amplitude_ratio.toFixed(3)}`}
          />
        ))
      }
      <LineChart
        title="Rayleigh-wave speed on the free surface"
        series={[{ name: 'error', x: ra.rows.map((r) => r.points_per_wavelength), y: ra.rows.map((r) => r.error_percent), color: 'var(--verdigris)', markers: true }]}
        xLabel="points per minimum S wavelength"
        yLabel="speed error"
        yFormat="pct"
        height={170}
        zeroLine
        yDomain={[-1.2, 0.2]}
        caption={`measured by cross-correlation at 3 to 8 wavelengths; exact ${ra.exact_m_s.toFixed(0)} m/s from the Rayleigh equation`}
      />
      <LineChart
        title="Energy left in the grid"
        series={[{ name: 'energy', x: ab.t_ms, y: ab.energy_relative, color: 'var(--verdigris)' }]}
        xLabel="time, ms"
        yLabel="relative energy"
        logY
        yFormat="exp"
        height={170}
        yDomain={[1e-10, 1]}
        caption={`C-PML, ${ab.pml_cells} cells on every face`}
      />
      <LineChart
        title={`Reciprocity across a ${rc.void_m} m void`}
        series={[
          { name: 'east force → up velocity', x: rc.t_ms, y: rc.forward, color: 'var(--text)', width: 1.5 },
          { name: 'up force → east velocity', x: rc.t_ms, y: rc.reverse, color: 'var(--verdigris)', dash: '4 3' },
        ]}
        xLabel="time, ms"
        yLabel="velocity (scaled)"
        height={170}
        zeroLine
        directLabels={false}
        caption={`correlation ${rc.ncc.toFixed(5)} · amplitude ratio ${rc.amplitude_ratio.toFixed(3)}`}
      />
    </div>
    <p class="runline mono">run {run.id} · {run.date} · commit {run.commit} · {run.runtime_s} s · {st.mcell_steps_per_s.toFixed(0)} M cell-steps/s on {run.environment.threads} threads</p>
  </section>

  <section id="traveltime">
    <h2>Travel times catch the chamber's shadow from boreholes, and nothing from the surface</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The one-chamber bench: a 6 m air-filled cube, its top 12 m down, in bare limestone. Every record comes from the
      solver above, with noise 30 dB below each record's peak; arrivals are picked by waveform correlation; the picks are
      inverted on a 1 m grid by a code that never sees the model. Air is {Math.round(-100 * cx.score.true_air_rel)}% slower
      than the rock; a first arrival goes around the void instead of through it, so what travel times can see is the
      delay of the detour.
    </p>
    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{pct(cx.score.chamber_mean_rel)}</span><span class="what">speed recovered inside the chamber from four boreholes</span><span class="src">crosshole · {cx.sources} shots, {cx.receivers} sensors</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{cx.score.contrast_to_noise.toFixed(1)}<small>×</small></span><span class="what">the chamber's anomaly against the tomogram's own scatter around it</span><span class="src">crosshole</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{pct(su.score.chamber_mean_rel)}</span><span class="what">speed recovered inside the chamber from the surface</span><span class="src">surface · {su.sources} shots, {su.receivers} geophones</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{(cx.pick_minus_straight_ms.p95_abs).toFixed(2)}<small>ms</small></span><span class="what">95th-percentile pick error against straight rays, the precision the picks carry</span><span class="src">crosshole picks</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="grid-3 charts">
      <SectionMap
        title={`Crosshole: vertical section through the chamber`}
        x={cx.slices.x}
        y={cx.slices.z}
        values={cx.slices.xz_rel}
        domain={0.08}
        outlines={[chamber]}
        points={holesXZ}
        xLabel="east, m"
        yLabel="height, m"
        caption={`relative P speed · sparker at ${tt.manifest.params.f0_hz.crosshole} Hz · boreholes at ±12 m`}
      />
      <SectionMap
        title="Crosshole: horizontal section at the chamber's centre"
        x={cx.slices.x}
        y={cx.slices.y.slice().reverse()}
        values={cx.slices.xy_rel.slice().reverse()}
        domain={0.08}
        outlines={[{ x0: -3, x1: 3, y0: -3, y1: 3 }]}
        points={[[-12, 0], [12, 0], [0, -12], [0, 12]]}
        xLabel="east, m"
        yLabel="north, m"
        caption="the four boreholes in ochre"
      />
      <SectionMap
        title="Surface: vertical section through the chamber"
        x={su.slices.x}
        y={su.slices.z}
        values={su.slices.xz_rel}
        domain={0.08}
        outlines={[chamber]}
        points={surfXZ}
        xLabel="east, m"
        yLabel="height, m"
        caption={`hammer at ${tt.manifest.params.f0_hz.surface} Hz · the picks time the surface wave (${Math.round(su.starting_speed_m_s).toLocaleString('en-US')} m/s): a vertical hammer's direct P along a free surface is weak; either way no first-arrival path reaches 12 m in uniform rock`}
      />
    </div>
    <p class="finding">{tt.finding}</p>
    <p class="runline mono">run {tt.manifest.id} · {tt.manifest.date} · commit {tt.manifest.commit} · {tt.manifest.runtime_s} s · <a href={`${base}/underworld/#bench-void`}>see the tomograms in the underworld</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="arrays">
    <h2>The arrays are the ones near-surface surveys actually deploy</h2>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Active</h3>
        <p>A grid of three-component geophones at 1 to 4 m spacing and a source walked across it (hammer or weight drop), with realistic source repeatability and sensor self-noise.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Passive</h3>
        <p>A dense nodal array recording the ambient hum of traffic, wind and visitors. It is the seismic cousin of the radar claim: natural vibration asked to reveal what lies below.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="methods">
    <h2>Three inversions, from the classic to the state of the art</h2>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Travel-time tomography</h3>
        <p>First arrivals picked from the records, bent rays traced through an eikonal solver, a regularised least-squares update. Robust, and blurred at a wavelength.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Ambient-noise tomography</h3>
        <p>Noise cross-correlated between sensor pairs becomes the wave that would travel between them; its dispersion is mapped and inverted for shear speed with depth.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <h3>Full-waveform inversion</h3>
        <p>The whole record fitted by adjoint-state gradients, from low frequencies up. The sharpest images near-surface geophysics makes, and the most demanding.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scoring">
    <h2>Detectability is a map over size, depth and array, not a yes or no</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      For each method and each chamber: the contrast recovered at the chamber against the spread of the background, the
      error in position and size, and the false chambers drawn elsewhere. Swept over chamber size, depth, array aperture,
      spacing and frequency, the result is a map of where seismology succeeds and where it stops, the ceiling against which
      the satellite is judged in Phase 2.
    </p>
  </section>
</Doc>

<style>
  .charts {
    margin-top: 18px;
    gap: 22px 28px;
  }
  .finding {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text);
    max-width: 780px;
    margin-top: 16px;
    padding-left: 12px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--verdigris);
  }
  .runline {
    font-size: 10px;
    color: var(--faint);
    margin-top: 14px;
  }
</style>
